# Deployment

## Environment variables
- Only `VITE_*` (Vite) or `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` (Next.js) reach the browser, and they're public. Secrets never use these prefixes.
- Public vars are inlined at build time: changing them on Vercel requires a redeploy.
- Set vars per Vercel environment (Production, Preview, Development). Preview should not write to the production database if avoidable.
- Keep `.env.example` up to date with every variable name. Read env vars in one module and fail loudly if a required one is missing.

## SPA routing
- Vite + React Router on Vercel needs this `vercel.json`, or deep links 404 on refresh:
  ```json
  { "rewrites": [{ "source": "/(.*)", "destination": "/index.html" }] }
  ```
- Netlify: `public/_redirects` containing `/*  /index.html  200`.
- Have a catch-all 404 route. Next.js needs no rewrite; use `app/not-found.tsx`.

## Build
- `npm run build` must pass locally before a change is considered done. Don't disable type checks or lint rules to get a green build.
- Test the production build (`npm run preview` / `npm run start`), not only the dev server.
- Lazy-load routes and heavy components to keep the initial bundle small.

## Auth URLs
- Supabase Site URL = production URL. Redirect URLs include production, preview (`https://*-<team>.vercel.app/**`) and localhost.
- After adding a custom domain, update Supabase Site URL, Redirect URLs and OAuth provider settings.
- Production needs custom SMTP for auth emails; the built-in sender is for testing.

## Errors
- App-level error boundary (`app/error.tsx` + `app/global-error.tsx` in Next.js; an ErrorBoundary around the router in Vite).
- Production error visibility (Sentry or similar, or Vercel logs for functions).
